// Pocket — the picture processing unit.
//
// Owns FF40-FF4B, FF4F, FF68-FF6C, the interpretation of GB_VRAM and GB_OAM,
// the tile-row decode cache, the framebuffer and the palette. Every byte it
// touches is declared in gb_state.logic; this file declares no storage.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT WAS PORTED, AND WHAT WAS NOT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The reference (emulator/src/emulator/core/ppu.ts) runs a per-dot pixel FIFO:
// a BG fetcher, a sprite fetcher and an output mixer, all stepped one dot at a
// time, 70224 times a frame. Transliterated into .logic that measured 13.8ms a
// frame — 83% of the whole 16.67ms budget, before the CPU or the APU ran.
//
// So the SCHEDULE is kept and the PIXEL PUSHING is not. Mode 2/3/0/1 lengths,
// LY, LYC, the STAT line and its four-cycle rising-edge delay, the line-153
// quirk and the LCD-off behaviour are all exactly the reference's, to the dot,
// because the CPU can see every one of them. What changed is that all 160
// pixels of a line are produced in one go at the mode 2 -> mode 3 edge, out of
// flat integer loops over typed arrays.
//
// The one behavioural cost is written down in CONTRACT.md §3.6: a mid-LINE
// write to SCX/SCY/LCDC/WX/WY no longer splits the line. Mid-FRAME writes work
// normally, so parallax, status bars and BGP fades are all intact; only
// per-pixel raster tricks inside a single scanline are lost.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THE ENGINE ACTUALLY CHARGES FOR — ALL MEASURED, NONE GUESSED
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every number below was measured on the shipping zipp WASM, and between them
// they decided the shape of every loop in this file.
//
//   a call, with an empty body ......................... ~17ns
//   a plain top-level number, read or written ........... ~2ns
//   ONE typed-array element, read or written ........... ~27ns
//   a typed-array method call (fill, set) .............. ~350ns
//   a statement, over and above what it computes ....... ~30ns
//
// Three consequences, and this file is built on them:
//
// 1. STATEMENTS COST MORE THAN ARITHMETIC. Splitting a value into a local so
//    it can be used twice measured SLOWER than reading the array again. The
//    per-pixel palette resolve is therefore one statement with the arithmetic
//    folded into it, and the eight pixels of a tile row are unrolled: dropping
//    the loop counter and its test took the background from 4.2ms to 3.2ms.
//
// 2. THE SCHEDULER IS CALLED 17,556 TIMES A FRAME. gb_machine ticks in
//    4-cycle chunks, so gbPpu_step runs more often than anything else in the
//    bundle. Written against GB_CTR it measured 17.7ms a frame — on its own,
//    more than the entire budget. Written as a countdown over two plain
//    numbers (GB_PPU_DOTS, GB_PPU_STATD — see the note in gb_state.logic) the
//    common call is a subtract and two compares, and everything that needs a
//    typed array happens at the ~600 mode boundaries instead of the 17,556
//    ticks. That single change is worth about 16ms a frame.
//
// 3. NOTHING PER-PIXEL MAY BE OPTIONAL. GB_LINE_BGCOLOR costs 1.3ms a frame to
//    export, so it is exported only when a sprite on that line actually has
//    its priority bit set (gbPpu_scanSprites works that out while it is
//    already reading OAM), and on CGB never — there the background colour is
//    recoverable from the framebuffer byte itself, because a CGB background
//    slot is palette*4 + colour and the low two bits ARE the colour.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHERE IT LANDED
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Per frame, on a Ryzen 9 9950X3D against the shipping zipp WASM, ticked in
// 4-cycle chunks exactly as gbMachine_tickParts will:
//
//                                        DMG       CGB
//   an ordinary game frame ............  4.8ms     5.6ms
//   ... plus 1KB of VRAM rewritten ....  5.0ms     5.9ms
//   ten sprites on every line, window,
//   256 distinct tiles, half of them
//   x-flipped .........................  6.3ms     7.0ms
//
// Of the ordinary frame's 4.8ms, 1.2ms is the cost of gbPpu_step being CALLED
// 17,556 times and returning — an empty function called that often costs the
// same. The PPU's own work is 3.6ms. gbPpu_step handles any chunk size (it
// loops), so if the whole-bundle budget ever needs that 1.2ms back,
// gbMachine_tickParts can hand the PPU larger chunks without changing a line
// here; the schedule stays dot-accurate either way, because everything it
// decides is driven off the countdown rather than off the call.
//
// The per-dot FIFO this replaced measured 13.8ms.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// FRAMES NOBODY SEES
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A frame emulated inside gbMachine_getSamples' catch-up is never the one
// getFrame presents — the next getFrame emulates and presents its own. While
// GB_PPU_SKIP is 1, gbPpu_advance calls gbPpu_skipLine instead of
// gbPpu_renderLine: the schedule is untouched, the window line counter is
// maintained, and the pixels are simply not produced. The VBlank swap is
// withheld for a frame with any skipped line, so GB_FB_PRESENT only ever holds
// a whole rendered frame. Measured on Pokémon Yellow: a catch-up call drops
// from 11.5ms to 8.1ms, and on a machine that cannot hold 60Hz about half the
// emulated frames are catch-up frames.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE TILE-ROW DECODE CACHE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A tile row is two bytes of interleaved bitplanes. Pulling one pixel out of
// it costs two shifts, two masks and an or, and doing that per pixel per
// scanline repeats the same eight extractions for every line that shares the
// row and every frame the tile does not change — which, for a background, is
// nearly all of them. gbPpu_ensureTileRow decodes a row into eight bytes ONCE
// and gb_mmu marks it dirty when the VRAM under it moves. In steady state a
// scanline does 21 dirty-flag reads and no decoding at all.
//
// The index arithmetic collapses to nothing because the cache is indexed by
// VRAM byte offset >> 1 across both banks:
//
//     row = tile * 8 + lineInTile          (+ 0x1000 for VRAM bank 1)
//
// and $8800 signed addressing is just two hoisted per-line constants — XOR the
// tile index with 0x80 and add 0x400 rows — so the whole per-tile address
// calculation is one statement with no branch:
//
//     row = ((GB_VRAM[mapAddr] ^ tileXor) << 3) + rowBias
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PALETTE RESOLUTION WITH NO BRANCH AND NO TABLE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// DMG resolves a colour through BGP/OBP as ((bgp >>> (c << 1)) & 3); CGB wants
// the raw colour with the palette number in the high bits. Rather than branch
// per pixel — or add a 64-entry lookup, which measured no faster than the
// arithmetic — both go through ONE expression:
//
//     GB_FB[i] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (c << 1)) & 3)
//
// DMG passes pshift = BGP/OBP0/OBP1 and pbase = 0/32/36. CGB passes
// pshift = 0xe4 — the identity permutation 11 10 01 00, so the shift and mask
// hand back c untouched — and pbase = the palette number times four. Both are
// hoisted per tile and per sprite, and the pixel loop never asks which machine
// it is on.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LAYER ORDER
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Background first, sprites second, and sprites drawn in strict descending
// priority so the first writer of a pixel always wins. That is what lets the
// background loop write unconditionally — it never has to ask whether a sprite
// is already there — and it means no sprite ever has to put a background pixel
// back. See gbPpu_drawSprites for why CGB's OPRI=0 needs its own traversal
// order to keep that invariant.

// ===========================================================================
// The notification from gb_mmu
//
// CONTRACT.md §4 fixes this body byte for byte, because gb_mmu is allowed to
// inline it and the two forms must not drift apart. flat is the VRAM offset
// across BOTH banks, (bank << 13) | (addr - 0x8000), and flat >>> 1 is exactly
// tile * 8 + row within the bank — the same index for both bytes of the pair,
// with no division and no branch. The cache covers the tilemap region too;
// that wastes 16KB and buys an invalidation with no bounds test at all.
// ===========================================================================

function gbPpu_dirtyVram(flat) {
  GB_TILEDIRTY[flat >>> 1] = 1
}

// Decode one 2bpp tile row into eight palette indices, left pixel first.
// Lazy: called at fetch time, never on write, because games blit far more VRAM
// bytes than they ever display. Unrolled — an inner loop over eight bits would
// cost more in loop overhead than the extractions themselves.
//
// X-flip is deliberately NOT cached separately; the drawing code reads these
// eight bytes backwards instead.
function gbPpu_ensureTileRow(row) {
  if (GB_TILEDIRTY[row] === 0) { return }
  let s = row << 1
  let lo = GB_VRAM[s]
  let hi = GB_VRAM[s + 1]
  let d = row << 3
  GB_TILECACHE[d] = (((hi >>> 7) & 1) << 1) | ((lo >>> 7) & 1)
  GB_TILECACHE[d + 1] = (((hi >>> 6) & 1) << 1) | ((lo >>> 6) & 1)
  GB_TILECACHE[d + 2] = (((hi >>> 5) & 1) << 1) | ((lo >>> 5) & 1)
  GB_TILECACHE[d + 3] = (((hi >>> 4) & 1) << 1) | ((lo >>> 4) & 1)
  GB_TILECACHE[d + 4] = (((hi >>> 3) & 1) << 1) | ((lo >>> 3) & 1)
  GB_TILECACHE[d + 5] = (((hi >>> 2) & 1) << 1) | ((lo >>> 2) & 1)
  GB_TILECACHE[d + 6] = (((hi >>> 1) & 1) << 1) | ((lo >>> 1) & 1)
  GB_TILECACHE[d + 7] = ((hi & 1) << 1) | (lo & 1)
  GB_TILEDIRTY[row] = 0
}

// ===========================================================================
// The STAT line
//
// STAT's interrupt is edge triggered on the OR of four enables plus the
// mode-2-on-VBlank-entry case, and it arrives four T-cycles after the line
// rises. GB_PPU_STATD is that countdown, -1 when idle. It is ticked in
// gbPpu_step rather than at mode boundaries so the interrupt still lands on
// the exact cycle even when it was armed by a register write mid-mode.
// ===========================================================================

function gbPpu_updateStatLine() {
  let stat = GB_IO[0x41]
  let mode = GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_MODE]
  let line = 0

  if ((stat & 0x40) !== 0 && (stat & 0x04) !== 0) {
    line = 1
  } else if ((stat & 0x20) !== 0 && mode === GB_MODE_OAM) {
    line = 1
  } else if ((stat & 0x10) !== 0 && mode === GB_MODE_VBLANK) {
    line = 1
  } else if ((stat & 0x08) !== 0 && mode === GB_MODE_HBLANK) {
    line = 1
  } else if ((stat & 0x20) !== 0 && mode === GB_MODE_VBLANK && GB_IO[0x44] === GB_SCREEN_H) {
    // The mode-2 source also fires on the VBlank entry line. Hardware quirk,
    // and Mooneye's stat_irq_blocking leans on it.
    line = 1
  }

  if (line === 1 && GB_CTR[GB_C_STAT_LINE] === 0) {
    GB_PPU_STATD = 4
    GB_CTR[GB_C_STAT_DELAY] = 4
  }
  GB_CTR[GB_C_STAT_LINE] = line
}

function gbPpu_checkLyc() {
  if (GB_IO[0x44] === GB_IO[0x45]) {
    GB_IO[0x41] = GB_IO[0x41] | 0x04
  } else {
    GB_IO[0x41] = GB_IO[0x41] & 0xfb
  }
  gbPpu_updateStatLine()
}

function gbPpu_setMode(m) {
  GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_MODE] = m
  GB_IO[0x41] = (GB_IO[0x41] & 0xfc) | (m & 0x03)
  gbPpu_updateStatLine()
}

// The LYC compare and the mode change together, for the transitions that do
// both — which is every one that ends a line. Running them as checkLyc() then
// setMode() costs two passes over the STAT sources for no benefit: both happen
// within the same dot, and the interrupt only cares about the rising edge of
// their OR, which is the same either way.
function gbPpu_lycMode(m) {
  let stat = (GB_IO[0x41] & 0xf8) | m
  if (GB_IO[0x44] === GB_IO[0x45]) { stat = stat | 0x04 }
  GB_IO[0x41] = stat
  GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_MODE] = m
  gbPpu_updateStatLine()
}

// ===========================================================================
// OAM scan — the ten sprites of this line
//
// Gathered in OAM order, which is what enforces the ten-sprite limit (the
// first ten that intersect the line win, not the leftmost ten), then insertion
// sorted by (x, oamIndex). The sort is stable because it only shifts past a
// STRICTLY greater x, so equal-x sprites keep their OAM order — and that pair
// is exactly the DMG priority rule, so drawing the sorted list first-writer-
// wins needs no further comparisons.
//
// The attribute byte is read here as well, purely to set GB_C_SPR_BGTEST: it
// is the cheapest place to find out whether any sprite on this line can lose
// to the background, and knowing that lets the background pass skip exporting
// GB_LINE_BGCOLOR, which is worth 1.3ms a frame.
// ===========================================================================

function gbPpu_scanSprites() {
  GB_CTR[GB_C_SPR_COUNT] = 0
  GB_CTR[GB_C_SPR_BGTEST] = 0
  let lcdc = GB_IO[0x40]
  if ((lcdc & 0x02) === 0) { return }

  let h = 8
  if ((lcdc & 0x04) !== 0) { h = 16 }
  let ly = GB_IO[0x44]
  let n = 0
  let bgtest = 0

  // Forty entries are examined on every one of the 144 lines, so the reject —
  // which is 39 of 40 of them — has to be a single test. (ly + 16 - y) is the
  // row within the sprite; shifting it into unsigned space folds the "above
  // the sprite" case into the "below it" one, so one comparison decides.
  let top = ly + 16

  for (let i = 0; i < 40 && n < GB_MAX_SPRITES_PER_LINE; i = i + 1) {
    let o = i << 2
    if ((top - GB_OAM[o]) >>> 0 < h) {
      let x = GB_OAM[o + 1]
      let k = n
      while (k > 0 && GB_SPR_X[k - 1] > x) {
        GB_SPR_X[k] = GB_SPR_X[k - 1]
        GB_SPR_IDX[k] = GB_SPR_IDX[k - 1]
        GB_SPR_Y[k] = GB_SPR_Y[k - 1]
        k = k - 1
      }
      GB_SPR_X[k] = x
      GB_SPR_IDX[k] = i
      GB_SPR_Y[k] = GB_OAM[o]
      n = n + 1
      if ((GB_OAM[o + 3] & 0x80) !== 0) { bgtest = 1 }
    }
  }

  GB_CTR[GB_C_SPR_COUNT] = n
  GB_CTR[GB_C_SPR_BGTEST] = bgtest
}

// ===========================================================================
// The background / window run
//
// Draws screen columns [x0, x1) from one tilemap row. The background and the
// window are the same code with different arguments: the background starts at
// column 0 with a sub-tile skip of SCX & 7 and wraps its map X at 31; the
// window starts at WX-7, tile aligned, from map column 0. Because the caller
// hands the window the columns the background does NOT get, the two runs never
// overlap and no pixel is drawn twice.
//
// A whole, unflipped tile — nineteen of the twenty-one on a line — goes
// through the unrolled block. The partial tiles at either end, and the CGB
// x-flipped ones, fall into the general loop below it. Everything constant
// across the line (tile addressing, BGP, machine type) is resolved before the
// loop; everything constant across a tile (cache base, palette, priority) is
// resolved once per tile.
//
// needBg is 1 only when a sprite on this line can lose to the background, in
// which case the raw colour has to be exported to GB_LINE_BGCOLOR as well.
// ===========================================================================

function gbPpu_drawTileRun(fbase, x0, x1, mapRowBase, tileX0, skip, rowInTile, needBg) {
  if (x0 >= x1) { return }

  let lcdc = GB_IO[0x40]
  let gbc = GB_CPUF[GB_P_GBC]

  // $8800 signed addressing as two constants: XOR the index with 0x80, then
  // add 0x400 rows. rowBiasFlip is the same for a CGB y-flipped tile.
  let tileXor = 0
  let rowBias = rowInTile
  let rowBiasFlip = 7 - rowInTile
  if ((lcdc & 0x10) === 0) {
    tileXor = 0x80
    rowBias = rowBias + 0x400
    rowBiasFlip = rowBiasFlip + 0x400
  }

  let pshift = GB_IO[0x47]
  let pbase = 0
  if (gbc === 1) { pshift = 0xe4 }

  let ma = mapRowBase + tileX0
  let rowEnd = mapRowBase + 32
  let x = x0
  let first = skip

  while (x < x1) {
    let row = 0
    let flip = 0
    let prio = 0

    if (gbc === 1) {
      let attr = GB_VRAM[0x2000 + ma]
      let rb = rowBias
      if ((attr & 0x40) !== 0) { rb = rowBiasFlip }
      if ((attr & 0x08) !== 0) { rb = rb + 0x1000 }
      row = ((GB_VRAM[ma] ^ tileXor) << 3) + rb
      pbase = (attr & 0x07) << 2
      flip = (attr >>> 5) & 1
      prio = (attr >>> 7) & 1
    } else {
      row = ((GB_VRAM[ma] ^ tileXor) << 3) + rowBias
    }

    if (GB_TILEDIRTY[row] !== 0) { gbPpu_ensureTileRow(row) }
    let cb = row << 3
    let xe = x + 8 - first
    if (xe > x1) { xe = x1 }

    if (flip === 0 && xe === x + 8) {
      // The whole eight-pixel row as two words. Cache bytes are 0-3, so the
      // unpack is (w >>> 8k) & 3 with no byte mask needed.
      let q = row << 1
      let f0 = fbase + x
      let w = GB_TILECACHE32[q]
      if (needBg === 0) {
        GB_FB[f0] = pbase | ((pshift >>> ((w & 3) << 1)) & 3)
        GB_FB[f0 + 1] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 8) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
        GB_FB[f0 + 2] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 16) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
        GB_FB[f0 + 3] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 24) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
        w = GB_TILECACHE32[q + 1]
        GB_FB[f0 + 4] = pbase | ((pshift >>> ((w & 3) << 1)) & 3)
        GB_FB[f0 + 5] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 8) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
        GB_FB[f0 + 6] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 16) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
        GB_FB[f0 + 7] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 24) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
      } else {
        GB_LINE_BGCOLOR[x] = w & 3
        GB_FB[f0] = pbase | ((pshift >>> ((w & 3) << 1)) & 3)
        GB_LINE_BGCOLOR[x + 1] = (w >>> 8) & 3
        GB_FB[f0 + 1] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 8) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
        GB_LINE_BGCOLOR[x + 2] = (w >>> 16) & 3
        GB_FB[f0 + 2] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 16) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
        GB_LINE_BGCOLOR[x + 3] = (w >>> 24) & 3
        GB_FB[f0 + 3] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 24) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
        w = GB_TILECACHE32[q + 1]
        GB_LINE_BGCOLOR[x + 4] = w & 3
        GB_FB[f0 + 4] = pbase | ((pshift >>> ((w & 3) << 1)) & 3)
        GB_LINE_BGCOLOR[x + 5] = (w >>> 8) & 3
        GB_FB[f0 + 5] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 8) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
        GB_LINE_BGCOLOR[x + 6] = (w >>> 16) & 3
        GB_FB[f0 + 6] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 16) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
        GB_LINE_BGCOLOR[x + 7] = (w >>> 24) & 3
        GB_FB[f0 + 7] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 24) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
      }
    } else if (flip === 1 && needBg === 0 && xe === x + 8) {
      // A whole x-flipped tile: the same two words, unpacked right to left.
      // needBg can never be 1 here — it is only ever set on DMG, and only CGB
      // has a flip bit — but the test keeps that from becoming a silent wrong
      // answer if that ever changes.
      let q = row << 1
      let f0 = fbase + x
      let w = GB_TILECACHE32[q + 1]
      GB_FB[f0] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 24) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
      GB_FB[f0 + 1] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 16) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
      GB_FB[f0 + 2] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 8) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
      GB_FB[f0 + 3] = pbase | ((pshift >>> ((w & 3) << 1)) & 3)
      w = GB_TILECACHE32[q]
      GB_FB[f0 + 4] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 24) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
      GB_FB[f0 + 5] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 16) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
      GB_FB[f0 + 6] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (((w >>> 8) & 3) << 1)) & 3)
      GB_FB[f0 + 7] = pbase | ((pshift >>> ((w & 3) << 1)) & 3)
    } else {
      // Partial tile at either end of the run, or a CGB x-flip. Always exports
      // the colour: at most sixteen pixels a line, so it is not worth a second
      // pair of loops to leave it out.
      let ci = cb + first
      let cstep = 1
      if (flip === 1) {
        ci = cb + 7 - first
        cstep = -1
      }
      for (let k = x; k < xe; k = k + 1) {
        GB_LINE_BGCOLOR[k] = GB_TILECACHE[ci]
        GB_FB[fbase + k] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (GB_LINE_BGCOLOR[k] << 1)) & 3)
        ci = ci + cstep
      }
    }

    // A native fill rather than an eight-iteration loop: the loop is eight
    // typed-array stores plus eight rounds of interpreted loop overhead, and
    // a CGB map row can have the priority bit on most of its tiles.
    if (prio === 1) { GB_LINE_BGPRIO.fill(1, x, xe) }

    x = xe
    first = 0
    ma = ma + 1
    if (ma >= rowEnd) { ma = mapRowBase }
  }
}

// ===========================================================================
// Sprites
//
// Drawn after the background, first-writer-wins through GB_LINE_SPROWNER. That
// only produces the right picture if the traversal order IS priority order:
//
//   DMG, and CGB with OPRI=1  — lower X wins, then lower OAM index. That is
//                               precisely the order gbPpu_scanSprites left the
//                               list in, so walk it straight through.
//   CGB with OPRI=0           — lower OAM index wins regardless of X. The list
//                               is sorted by X, so walk it by ascending OAM
//                               index instead. Ten entries, so a selection
//                               walk beats re-sorting, and it only happens on
//                               CGB carts that clear OPRI.
//
// Doing it this way rather than last-writer-wins matters for a real case: two
// overlapping sprites where the higher-priority one has its BG-priority bit
// set over non-zero background. It has to lose to the BACKGROUND, not to the
// lower-priority sprite underneath it — and since a losing sprite here simply
// never writes, the background pixel already in GB_FB stands.
//
// pmode is the background test, resolved once per sprite:
//   0  the sprite always wins (DMG with the priority bit clear, or CGB with
//      LCDC.0 clear, where the background has no priority at all)
//   1  DMG, priority bit set: the background wins wherever it is not colour 0,
//      read from GB_LINE_BGCOLOR
//   2  CGB, priority bit clear: the background wins where it is not colour 0
//      AND its tile attribute claims priority
//   3  CGB, priority bit set: the background wins wherever it is not colour 0
//
// On CGB the background colour is not exported at all — a CGB background slot
// is palette*4 + colour, so GB_FB[i] & 3 IS the colour, and at the moment this
// test runs GB_FB still holds the background pixel.
// ===========================================================================

// Does this sprite pixel beat the background under it? Only reached for a
// sprite that can actually lose — pmode 0 never calls here — so it is off the
// hot path by construction.
//   1  DMG with the priority bit set: the background wins wherever it is not
//      colour 0, taken from GB_LINE_BGCOLOR
//   2  CGB with the priority bit clear: the background wins where it is not
//      colour 0 AND its tile attribute claims priority
//   3  CGB with the priority bit set: the background wins wherever it is not
//      colour 0
// On CGB the background colour is not exported at all — a CGB background slot
// is palette*4 + colour, so the low two bits of the framebuffer byte ARE the
// colour, and GB_FB still holds the background pixel at this point.
function gbPpu_spriteWins(pmode, fi, x) {
  if (pmode === 1) { return GB_LINE_BGCOLOR[x] === 0 }
  if ((GB_FB[fi] & 3) === 0) { return true }
  return pmode === 2 && GB_LINE_BGPRIO[x] === 0
}

function gbPpu_drawSprites(fbase, ly) {
  let lcdc = GB_IO[0x40]
  if ((lcdc & 0x02) === 0) { return }
  let count = GB_CTR[GB_C_SPR_COUNT]
  if (count === 0) { return }

  GB_LINE_SPROWNER.fill(0)

  let gbc = GB_CPUF[GB_P_GBC]
  let h = 8
  if ((lcdc & 0x04) !== 0) { h = 16 }

  let masterOff = 0
  if (gbc === 1 && (lcdc & 0x01) === 0) { masterOff = 1 }
  let oamPriority = 0
  if (gbc === 1 && GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_OPRI] === 0) { oamPriority = 1 }

  let obp0 = GB_IO[0x48]
  let obp1 = GB_IO[0x49]
  let prevIdx = -1

  for (let s = 0; s < count; s = s + 1) {
    let e = s

    if (oamPriority === 1) {
      let best = -1
      let bestIdx = 256
      for (let j = 0; j < count; j = j + 1) {
        let ij = GB_SPR_IDX[j]
        if (ij > prevIdx && ij < bestIdx) {
          bestIdx = ij
          best = j
        }
      }
      if (best < 0) { break }
      e = best
      prevIdx = bestIdx
    }

    let sx = GB_SPR_X[e] - 8
    // X of 0, or of 168 and up, puts every pixel off screen. Hardware still
    // spends the sprite's slot on the line, which is why the count — and so
    // the mode 3 length — was taken before this test.
    if (sx <= -8 || sx >= GB_SCREEN_W) { continue }

    let idx = GB_SPR_IDX[e]
    let o = idx << 2
    let attr = GB_OAM[o + 3]
    let row = ly - (GB_SPR_Y[e] - 16)
    if ((attr & 0x40) !== 0) { row = h - 1 - row }
    let tile = GB_OAM[o + 2]
    if (h === 16) { tile = tile & 0xfe }

    // Sprite tile data is always unsigned from 0x0000 of its bank, and an 8x16
    // sprite's lower half is simply the next tile — which (row >>> 3) picks up
    // without a branch.
    let crow = ((tile + (row >>> 3)) << 3) + (row & 7)

    let pshift = 0xe4
    let pbase = 32
    let pmode = 0

    if (gbc === 1) {
      if ((attr & 0x08) !== 0) { crow = crow + 0x1000 }
      pbase = 32 + ((attr & 0x07) << 2)
      if (masterOff === 0) {
        pmode = 2
        if ((attr & 0x80) !== 0) { pmode = 3 }
      }
    } else {
      if ((attr & 0x10) !== 0) {
        pbase = 36
        pshift = obp1
      } else {
        pshift = obp0
      }
      if ((attr & 0x80) !== 0) { pmode = 1 }
    }

    if (GB_TILEDIRTY[crow] !== 0) { gbPpu_ensureTileRow(crow) }
    let cb = crow << 3

    let xs = sx
    let xe = sx + 8
    if (xs < 0) { xs = 0 }
    if (xe > GB_SCREEN_W) { xe = GB_SCREEN_W }
    let owner = idx + 1

    // The cache index is folded into the screen X so the loop carries one
    // counter instead of two, and x-flip only changes its sign. pmode is
    // constant for the whole sprite, so the background test is lifted out of
    // the loop entirely rather than re-decided eight times: a sprite that
    // cannot lose to the background — most of them on DMG — runs a loop with
    // no background reads in it at all.
    let cbase = cb - sx
    let step = 1
    if ((attr & 0x20) !== 0) {
      cbase = cb + 7 + sx
      step = -1
    }

    if (pmode === 0) {
      if (step === 1) {
        for (let x = xs; x < xe; x = x + 1) {
          if (GB_TILECACHE[cbase + x] !== 0 && GB_LINE_SPROWNER[x] === 0) {
            GB_LINE_SPROWNER[x] = owner
            GB_FB[fbase + x] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (GB_TILECACHE[cbase + x] << 1)) & 3)
          }
        }
      } else {
        for (let x = xs; x < xe; x = x + 1) {
          if (GB_TILECACHE[cbase - x] !== 0 && GB_LINE_SPROWNER[x] === 0) {
            GB_LINE_SPROWNER[x] = owner
            GB_FB[fbase + x] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (GB_TILECACHE[cbase - x] << 1)) & 3)
          }
        }
      }
    } else if (step === 1) {
      for (let x = xs; x < xe; x = x + 1) {
        if (GB_TILECACHE[cbase + x] !== 0 && GB_LINE_SPROWNER[x] === 0) {
          GB_LINE_SPROWNER[x] = owner
          if (gbPpu_spriteWins(pmode, fbase + x, x)) {
            GB_FB[fbase + x] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (GB_TILECACHE[cbase + x] << 1)) & 3)
          }
        }
      }
    } else {
      for (let x = xs; x < xe; x = x + 1) {
        if (GB_TILECACHE[cbase - x] !== 0 && GB_LINE_SPROWNER[x] === 0) {
          GB_LINE_SPROWNER[x] = owner
          if (gbPpu_spriteWins(pmode, fbase + x, x)) {
            GB_FB[fbase + x] = pbase | ((pshift >>> (GB_TILECACHE[cbase - x] << 1)) & 3)
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// ===========================================================================
// One scanline, all at once
//
// Called at the mode 2 -> mode 3 edge, after gbPpu_scanSprites, so LY, the
// sprite list and every register are the ones the line started with.
// ===========================================================================

// The schedule's share of gbPpu_renderLine and none of the pixels. Everything a
// later line or frame can observe has to come out the same: the early return
// for a line off the bottom, GB_C_WINDOW_SEEN (which gbPpu_advance turns into
// the window line counter at the end of mode 3), and the window test itself,
// clause for clause. Sprite scanning has already happened — it sets the mode
// 3 length and is done by the caller either way.
function gbPpu_skipLine() {
  gbPpu_skipped = 1
  let ly = GB_IO[0x44]
  if (ly >= GB_SCREEN_H) { return }
  GB_CTR[GB_C_WINDOW_SEEN] = 0
  let lcdc = GB_IO[0x40]
  if ((lcdc & 0x20) !== 0) {
    let wx = GB_IO[0x4b]
    if (ly >= GB_IO[0x4a] && wx >= 7 && wx <= 166 && wx - 7 < GB_SCREEN_W) {
      GB_CTR[GB_C_WINDOW_SEEN] = 1
    }
  }
}

function gbPpu_renderLine() {
  let ly = GB_IO[0x44]
  if (ly >= GB_SCREEN_H) { return }

  let lcdc = GB_IO[0x40]
  let gbc = GB_CPUF[GB_P_GBC]
  let fbase = ly * GB_SCREEN_W

  GB_CTR[GB_C_WINDOW_SEEN] = 0

  // On DMG the attribute priority does not exist and this array stays zero
  // from gbState_reset onwards, so only CGB pays for the clear.
  if (gbc === 1) { GB_LINE_BGPRIO.fill(0) }

  // The background only has to export its raw colour when a sprite on this
  // line can lose to it — and never on CGB, where GB_FB & 3 is the colour.
  let needBg = 0
  if (
    gbc === 0 && GB_CTR[GB_C_SPR_BGTEST] === 1 &&
    (lcdc & 0x02) !== 0 && GB_CTR[GB_C_SPR_COUNT] !== 0
  ) {
    needBg = 1
  }

  // Where the window takes over, if it does. The reference triggers the window
  // fetcher when the output X reaches WX-7, which can only happen for WX in
  // 7..166; WX below 7 never triggers. This is decided even when the
  // background is switched off, because the window line counter advances on
  // any line the window was triggered on.
  let winStart = GB_SCREEN_W
  if ((lcdc & 0x20) !== 0) {
    let wx = GB_IO[0x4b]
    if (ly >= GB_IO[0x4a] && wx >= 7 && wx <= 166) {
      let ws = wx - 7
      if (ws < GB_SCREEN_W) {
        winStart = ws
        GB_CTR[GB_C_WINDOW_SEEN] = 1
      }
    }
  }

  if (gbc === 0 && (lcdc & 0x01) === 0) {
    // DMG with LCDC.0 clear: background AND window are blank. Colour 0 still
    // goes through BGP, and it still counts as colour 0 for sprite priority.
    GB_FB.fill(GB_IO[0x47] & 0x03, fbase, fbase + GB_SCREEN_W)
    GB_LINE_BGCOLOR.fill(0)
  } else {
    if (winStart > 0) {
      let scx = GB_IO[0x43]
      let bgY = (ly + GB_IO[0x42]) & 0xff
      let mapBase = 0x1800
      if ((lcdc & 0x08) !== 0) { mapBase = 0x1c00 }
      gbPpu_drawTileRun(
        fbase, 0, winStart,
        mapBase + (((bgY >>> 3) & 31) << 5),
        (scx >>> 3) & 31, scx & 7, bgY & 7, needBg
      )
    }
    if (winStart < GB_SCREEN_W) {
      let wl = GB_CTR[GB_C_WINDOW_LINE]
      let mapBase = 0x1800
      if ((lcdc & 0x40) !== 0) { mapBase = 0x1c00 }
      gbPpu_drawTileRun(
        fbase, winStart, GB_SCREEN_W,
        mapBase + (((wl >>> 3) & 31) << 5),
        0, 0, wl & 7, needBg
      )
    }
  }

  gbPpu_drawSprites(fbase, ly)
}

// ===========================================================================
// The palette the host paints with
//
// GB_FB holds palette SLOTS, not colours: 0-31 background, 32-63 sprite, four
// colours each. gbPpu_buildPalette turns the machine's palette state into the
// 64 x RGB table PixelCanvas uses, once a frame at the VBlank edge, and bumps
// GB_C_PAL_REV only when a byte actually moved so the canvas can skip
// rebuilding its lookup.
//
// DMG resolves BGP/OBP at the moment each pixel is written, not here, so its
// slots are shades and this table is just the user's chosen LCD colours.
// ===========================================================================

function gbPpu_storeRgb(slot, r, g, b) {
  let d = slot * 3
  if (GB_PAL_RGB[d] === r && GB_PAL_RGB[d + 1] === g && GB_PAL_RGB[d + 2] === b) {
    return 0
  }
  GB_PAL_RGB[d] = r
  GB_PAL_RGB[d + 1] = g
  GB_PAL_RGB[d + 2] = b
  return 1
}

// BGR555, little endian, expanded with the standard (x << 3) | (x >> 2) so
// 0x1f reaches 0xff rather than 0xf8.
function gbPpu_storeCgb(slot, lo, hi) {
  let v = lo | (hi << 8)
  let r = v & 31
  let g = (v >>> 5) & 31
  let b = (v >>> 10) & 31
  return gbPpu_storeRgb(
    slot,
    (r << 3) | (r >>> 2),
    (g << 3) | (g >>> 2),
    (b << 3) | (b >>> 2)
  )
}

function gbPpu_buildPalette() {
  let changed = 0

  if (GB_CPUF[GB_P_GBC] === 1) {
    for (let p = 0; p < 8; p = p + 1) {
      let pb = p << 3
      let sb = p << 2
      for (let c = 0; c < 4; c = c + 1) {
        let i = pb + (c << 1)
        changed = changed | gbPpu_storeCgb(sb + c, GB_CGB_BGPAL[i], GB_CGB_BGPAL[i + 1])
        changed = changed | gbPpu_storeCgb(32 + sb + c, GB_CGB_OBJPAL[i], GB_CGB_OBJPAL[i + 1])
      }
    }
  } else {
    // Surface 0 is the background, 1 is OBP0, 2 is OBP1. Everything else is
    // black — the DMG only ever occupies ten of the sixty-four slots.
    for (let s = 0; s < 64; s = s + 1) {
      let src = -1
      if (s < 4) {
        src = s * 3
      } else if (s >= 32 && s < 36) {
        src = (4 + (s - 32)) * 3
      } else if (s >= 36 && s < 40) {
        src = (8 + (s - 36)) * 3
      }
      if (src < 0) {
        changed = changed | gbPpu_storeRgb(s, 0, 0, 0)
      } else {
        changed = changed | gbPpu_storeRgb(
          s, GB_DMG_SHADES[src], GB_DMG_SHADES[src + 1], GB_DMG_SHADES[src + 2]
        )
      }
    }
  }

  if (changed !== 0) {
    GB_CTR[GB_C_PAL_REV] = GB_CTR[GB_C_PAL_REV] + 1
  }
}

// Thirty-six bytes: three surfaces (BG, OBP0, OBP1) of four shades of RGB.
// Cold path — the user picking an LCD colour, not anything per frame.
function gbPpu_setDmgShades(b64) {
  if (b64 === undefined || b64 === null || b64 === "") { return false }
  let bytes = Uint8Array.fromBase64(b64)
  if (bytes.length < 36) { return false }
  for (let i = 0; i < 36; i = i + 1) {
    GB_DMG_SHADES[i] = bytes[i]
  }
  gbPpu_buildPalette()
  return true
}

// ===========================================================================
// Mode timing
//
// Dot accurate, because the CPU can see all of it — but arranged as a
// countdown rather than an elapsed counter, because gb_machine calls
// gbPpu_step 17,556 times a frame and the ordinary call must therefore be
// almost free. GB_PPU_DOTS holds the T-cycles left in the current mode and
// goes negative by the overshoot when a chunk crosses a boundary;
// gbPpu_advance carries that spill into the next mode, so nothing is lost and
// nothing has to be reconstructed.
//
// Mode 3 is 172 + (SCX & 7) + 6 per sprite on the line, clamped to 172..289,
// and HBlank is whatever is left of the 456. The whole scanline is drawn at
// the mode 2 -> 3 edge, so no dot inside mode 3 needs a decision.
// ===========================================================================

// Seventeen thousand five hundred and fifty-six calls a frame go through
// these two statements and nothing else. Both conditions are folded into one
// test — a second `if` measured 0.35ms a frame on its own — and everything
// that needs a typed array happens in gbPpu_tickEvent, which runs at the ~600
// mode boundaries and the handful of STAT deliveries instead.
function gbPpu_step(cycles) {
  GB_PPU_DOTS = GB_PPU_DOTS - cycles
  if (GB_PPU_DOTS <= 0 || GB_PPU_STATD > 0) { gbPpu_tickEvent(cycles) }
}

function gbPpu_tickEvent(cycles) {
  if (GB_PPU_STATD > 0) {
    GB_PPU_STATD = GB_PPU_STATD - cycles
    if (GB_PPU_STATD <= 0) {
      GB_PPU_STATD = -1
      GB_CTR[GB_C_STAT_DELAY] = -1
      if (GB_CTR[GB_C_STAT_LINE] === 1) { gbMmu_requestIrq(GB_IRQ_STAT) }
    }
  }
  if (GB_PPU_DOTS <= 0) { gbPpu_advance() }
}

// While the LCD is off nothing advances: LY reads 0, the mode reads 0, and the
// countdown is parked far enough out that gbPpu_advance effectively never runs
// again until something turns the LCD back on.
function gbPpu_lcdOff() {
  GB_IO[0x44] = 0
  GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_MODE] = GB_MODE_HBLANK
  GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_DOT] = 0
  GB_IO[0x41] = GB_IO[0x41] & 0xfc
  GB_PPU_DOTS = 0x40000000
}

function gbPpu_advance() {
  while (GB_PPU_DOTS <= 0) {
    if ((GB_IO[0x40] & 0x80) === 0) {
      gbPpu_lcdOff()
      return
    }

    let mode = GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_MODE]

    if (mode === GB_MODE_OAM) {
      gbPpu_scanSprites()
      let len = 172 + (GB_IO[0x43] & 7) + 6 * GB_CTR[GB_C_SPR_COUNT]
      if (len > 289) { len = 289 }
      GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_MODE3] = len
      GB_PPU_DOTS = GB_PPU_DOTS + len
      if (GB_PPU_SKIP === 0) {
        gbPpu_renderLine()
      } else {
        gbPpu_skipLine()
      }
      gbPpu_setMode(GB_MODE_PIXEL)

    } else if (mode === GB_MODE_PIXEL) {
      GB_PPU_DOTS = GB_PPU_DOTS + (GB_SCANLINE_DOTS - GB_MODE2_DOTS - GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_MODE3])
      if (GB_CTR[GB_C_WINDOW_SEEN] === 1) {
        GB_CTR[GB_C_WINDOW_LINE] = GB_CTR[GB_C_WINDOW_LINE] + 1
      }
      gbPpu_setMode(GB_MODE_HBLANK)
      gbMmu_hblankHdma()

    } else if (mode === GB_MODE_HBLANK) {
      GB_IO[0x44] = (GB_IO[0x44] + 1) & 0xff
      if (GB_IO[0x44] === GB_SCREEN_H) {
        GB_PPU_DOTS = GB_PPU_DOTS + GB_SCANLINE_DOTS
        gbPpu_lycMode(GB_MODE_VBLANK)
        GB_PPU_FRAME = GB_PPU_FRAME + 1
        GB_CTR[GB_C_FRAME] = GB_PPU_FRAME
        // The frame the host sees is the last COMPLETE one. Encoding the live
        // buffer would tear every time the host's rAF and the emulated
        // 59.7275Hz drift past each other, which they do continuously.
        //
        // The two buffers are SWAPPED rather than copied. A 23040-byte
        // .set() measured 0.686ms — a twentieth of the frame budget for one
        // memcpy — and exchanging two references costs nothing. It is sound
        // because every one of the 144 lines is rewritten in full on every
        // frame the LCD is on, so the buffer being drawn into is completely
        // replaced before it is presented again.
        // A frame with a skipped line is not a picture; keep presenting the
        // last one that is. See FRAMES NOBODY SEES above.
        if (gbPpu_skipped === 0) {
          let swap = GB_FB
          GB_FB = GB_FB_PRESENT
          GB_FB_PRESENT = swap
        }
        gbPpu_skipped = 0
        gbPpu_buildPalette()
        gbMmu_requestIrq(GB_IRQ_VBLANK)
      } else {
        GB_PPU_DOTS = GB_PPU_DOTS + GB_MODE2_DOTS
        gbPpu_lycMode(GB_MODE_OAM)
      }

    } else if (GB_CTR[GB_C_LINE153] === 1) {
      // The last 452 dots of line 153 are over; the frame restarts with LY
      // already reading 0.
      GB_CTR[GB_C_LINE153] = 0
      GB_PPU_DOTS = GB_PPU_DOTS + GB_MODE2_DOTS
      gbPpu_setMode(GB_MODE_OAM)

    } else if (GB_IO[0x44] === 153) {
      // The line-153 quirk: four dots in, LY starts reading 0 for the rest of
      // the frame while the PPU is still on line 153.
      GB_IO[0x44] = 0
      GB_CTR[GB_C_WINDOW_LINE] = 0
      GB_CTR[GB_C_LINE153] = 1
      GB_PPU_DOTS = GB_PPU_DOTS + (GB_SCANLINE_DOTS - 4)
      gbPpu_checkLyc()

    } else {
      GB_IO[0x44] = (GB_IO[0x44] + 1) & 0xff
      if (GB_IO[0x44] >= GB_TOTAL_LINES) {
        // Only reachable if something wrote LY out from under us.
        GB_IO[0x44] = 0
        GB_CTR[GB_C_WINDOW_LINE] = 0
        GB_CTR[GB_C_LINE153] = 0
        GB_PPU_DOTS = GB_PPU_DOTS + GB_MODE2_DOTS
        gbPpu_lycMode(GB_MODE_OAM)
      } else if (GB_IO[0x44] === 153) {
        GB_PPU_DOTS = GB_PPU_DOTS + 4
        gbPpu_checkLyc()
      } else {
        GB_PPU_DOTS = GB_PPU_DOTS + GB_SCANLINE_DOTS
        gbPpu_checkLyc()
      }
    }

    // The cold mirror, for save states and the debugger. GB_PPU_DOTS is the
    // live counter; this is what is left of the mode as of this boundary.
    GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_DOT] = GB_PPU_DOTS
  }
}

// ===========================================================================
// Registers
//
// gb_mmu forwards FF40-FF4B (bar FF46, which is its own), FF4F and FF68-FF6C.
// An if-chain rather than a switch — zipp compiles switch to a linear scan
// anyway — ordered by how often a game touches each: LY and STAT are polled in
// tight loops, OPRI is written once ever.
// ===========================================================================

function gbPpu_readReg(addr) {
  if (addr === 0xff44) { return GB_IO[0x44] }
  if (addr === 0xff41) { return GB_IO[0x41] | 0x80 }
  if (addr === 0xff40) { return GB_IO[0x40] }
  if (addr === 0xff42) { return GB_IO[0x42] }
  if (addr === 0xff43) { return GB_IO[0x43] }
  if (addr === 0xff45) { return GB_IO[0x45] }
  if (addr === 0xff47) { return GB_IO[0x47] }
  if (addr === 0xff48) { return GB_IO[0x48] }
  if (addr === 0xff49) { return GB_IO[0x49] }
  if (addr === 0xff4a) { return GB_IO[0x4a] }
  if (addr === 0xff4b) { return GB_IO[0x4b] }

  // CGB only below here; on a DMG cart every one of these is open bus.
  if (GB_CPUF[GB_P_GBC] !== 1) { return 0xff }

  if (addr === 0xff4f) { return GB_CTR[GB_C_VRAM_BANK] | 0xfe }
  if (addr === 0xff68) { return GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_BGPAL_IDX] | 0x40 }
  if (addr === 0xff69) { return GB_CGB_BGPAL[GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_BGPAL_IDX] & 0x3f] }
  if (addr === 0xff6a) { return GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_OBJPAL_IDX] | 0x40 }
  if (addr === 0xff6b) { return GB_CGB_OBJPAL[GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_OBJPAL_IDX] & 0x3f] }
  if (addr === 0xff6c) { return GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_OPRI] | 0xfe }

  return 0xff
}

function gbPpu_writeReg(addr, v) {
  let b = v & 0xff

  if (addr === 0xff40) {
    let wasOn = (GB_IO[0x40] & 0x80) !== 0
    GB_IO[0x40] = b
    let isOn = (b & 0x80) !== 0
    if (wasOn !== isOn) {
      // Both edges restart the PPU from line 0 in mode 0. Coming back on, real
      // hardware spends a warm-up before the first mode 2; entering mode 0
      // with a zeroed counter approximates it the way the reference does,
      // which is what Mooneye's lcdon_timing expects.
      GB_IO[0x44] = 0
      GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_MODE] = GB_MODE_HBLANK
      GB_CTR[GB_C_WINDOW_LINE] = 0
      GB_CTR[GB_C_WINDOW_SEEN] = 0
      GB_CTR[GB_C_LINE153] = 0
      GB_CTR[GB_C_STAT_LINE] = 0
      GB_CTR[GB_C_STAT_DELAY] = -1
      GB_PPU_STATD = -1
      GB_IO[0x41] = GB_IO[0x41] & 0xfc
      if (isOn) {
        GB_PPU_DOTS = GB_SCANLINE_DOTS - GB_MODE2_DOTS - GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_MODE3]
        GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_DOT] = GB_PPU_DOTS
        gbPpu_checkLyc()
      } else {
        gbPpu_lcdOff()
      }
    }
    return
  }

  if (addr === 0xff41) {
    // Bits 0-2 are the PPU's own; only the four enables are writable.
    GB_IO[0x41] = (b & 0x78) | (GB_IO[0x41] & 0x07)
    gbPpu_updateStatLine()
    return
  }

  if (addr === 0xff42) { GB_IO[0x42] = b; return }
  if (addr === 0xff43) { GB_IO[0x43] = b; return }

  // A cart write to LY resets it, exactly as the reference has it — no LYC
  // re-check, because the hardware compare happens on the PPU's own increment.
  if (addr === 0xff44) { GB_IO[0x44] = 0; return }

  if (addr === 0xff45) {
    GB_IO[0x45] = b
    gbPpu_checkLyc()
    return
  }

  // DMG palettes are resolved at pixel-write time, so a mid-frame change to
  // any of these needs no recomputation here — the rest of the frame simply
  // comes out with the new mapping, which is the whole point.
  if (addr === 0xff47) { GB_IO[0x47] = b; return }
  if (addr === 0xff48) { GB_IO[0x48] = b; return }
  if (addr === 0xff49) { GB_IO[0x49] = b; return }
  if (addr === 0xff4a) { GB_IO[0x4a] = b; return }
  if (addr === 0xff4b) { GB_IO[0x4b] = b; return }

  if (GB_CPUF[GB_P_GBC] !== 1) { return }

  if (addr === 0xff4f) { GB_CTR[GB_C_VRAM_BANK] = b & 1; return }

  if (addr === 0xff68) { GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_BGPAL_IDX] = b & 0xbf; return }
  if (addr === 0xff69) {
    let ix = GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_BGPAL_IDX]
    GB_CGB_BGPAL[ix & 0x3f] = b
    if ((ix & 0x80) !== 0) {
      GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_BGPAL_IDX] = 0x80 | ((ix + 1) & 0x3f)
    }
    return
  }

  if (addr === 0xff6a) { GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_OBJPAL_IDX] = b & 0xbf; return }
  if (addr === 0xff6b) {
    let ix = GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_OBJPAL_IDX]
    GB_CGB_OBJPAL[ix & 0x3f] = b
    if ((ix & 0x80) !== 0) {
      GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_OBJPAL_IDX] = 0x80 | ((ix + 1) & 0x3f)
    }
    return
  }

  if (addr === 0xff6c) { GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_OPRI] = b & 1; return }
}

// ===========================================================================
// Post-boot state
//
// gbState_reset zeroes GB_IO, which leaves LCDC at 0 — an LCD that is switched
// off and a machine that never produces a frame. Pocket starts post-boot, so
// somebody has to put the boot ROM's parting values back. gb_machine's reset
// must call this after gbState_reset and before gbCpu_reset.
// ===========================================================================

function gbPpu_reset() {
  GB_PPU_SKIP = 0
  gbPpu_skipped = 0
  GB_IO[0x40] = 0x91
  GB_IO[0x41] = 0x85
  GB_IO[0x42] = 0
  GB_IO[0x43] = 0
  GB_IO[0x44] = 0
  GB_IO[0x45] = 0
  GB_IO[0x47] = 0xfc
  GB_IO[0x48] = 0xff
  GB_IO[0x49] = 0xff
  GB_IO[0x4a] = 0
  GB_IO[0x4b] = 0

  GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_MODE] = GB_MODE_OAM
  GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_MODE3] = 172
  GB_CTR[GB_C_STAT_LINE] = 0
  GB_CTR[GB_C_STAT_DELAY] = -1
  GB_CTR[GB_C_WINDOW_LINE] = 0
  GB_CTR[GB_C_WINDOW_SEEN] = 0
  GB_CTR[GB_C_LINE153] = 0
  GB_CTR[GB_C_SPR_COUNT] = 0
  GB_CTR[GB_C_SPR_BGTEST] = 0
  GB_CTR[GB_C_VRAM_BANK] = 0
  GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_BGPAL_IDX] = 0
  GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_OBJPAL_IDX] = 0
  // OPRI defaults to 1 — first-writer-wins, the DMG rule — until a CGB game
  // says otherwise.
  GB_CTR[GB_C_CGB_OPRI] = 1

  GB_PPU_DOTS = GB_MODE2_DOTS
  GB_PPU_STATD = -1
  GB_CTR[GB_C_PPU_DOT] = GB_PPU_DOTS

  GB_IO[0x41] = (GB_IO[0x41] & 0xfc) | GB_MODE_OAM
  gbPpu_buildPalette()
}
